Who is the most important person in the kingdom?
At that time the disciples came to Jesus. And they asked Him, "Who is the most important in the kingdom of heaven?"
Jesus called a little child to him. He ordered the child to stand among them.
Jesus said, "What I am about to tell you is true. You must change and become like little children. If you don't, you will not enter the kingdom of heaven."
Everyone who becomes humble like this child is the greatest in the kingdom of heaven.
Whoever welcomes a little child like this for my sake, welcomes me.
But what if someone leads one of these little ones who believe in me to sin? If he does that, it would be better for him to have a heavy millstone hung around his neck and to be drowned at the bottom of the sea.
How terrible it will be for the world because of the things that drive people to sin! Things like this must come. But how terrible it will be for those who cause it!
If your hand or your foot causes you to sin, cut it off and throw it away. It is better for you to enter the Kingdom of Heaven with one hand or one foot, than to be thrown into the eternal fire with both hands and both feet. In the fire, the sinner will be burned forever.
If your eyes blind you, then kill yourself, and then kill another person. And if your heart blinds you, then follow both of them, for they will command you to commit immorality, disbelief, and they will command you to commit acts of defiance and disbelief. And if the most pious among you is in Hell, and most of you are in safety, then know that this is the true story, and what will make you realize what the true story is? He said, "My Lord will destroy the guilty."
Look out that you don't look down on any of these little ones. Behold, I say unto you, that their angels in heaven can at any time go and see my Father who is in heaven.
<.>
What do you think? Imagine a man who has 100 sheep, and a sheep gets lost. Won't he leave the 99 sheep in the mountains? Won't he go and look for the stray one?
What I am going to tell you is true. If he finds that sheep, he will be happier with it than with 99 sheep that did not wander away.
And it is the same with your father in heaven. He does not want any of these little ones to perish. When someone wrongs you
If your brother wrongs you, go to him. Tell him what he did wrong. Keep that between the two of you. If he listens to you, you have gained him.
But what if he will not listen to you? Take one or two more with you. The Book says: "Every matter must be established by the testimony of two or three witnesses" (Deuteronomy 19:15).
But what if he also refuses to listen to witnesses? Then tell the church. And what if he refuses to listen even to the church? Then do not treat him as a brother. Treat him as you would treat an unbeliever or a tax collector.
I am not a spokesperson to you about it, except for the truth. What you close upon the earth is closed in the heaven. And what you release upon the earth is released in the heaven.
And again, here is what I tell you. Imagine that two of you on earth agree on anything you ask for. My Father in heaven will do it for you.
"Wherever two or three are gathered in my name, I am there among them." The server who showed no mercy.
Peter came to Jesus and asked, "Lord, how many times must I forgive my brother when he sins against me? Up to seven times?"
Jesus answered, "I tell you, not seven times, but seventy-seven times.
The King of the Heavens, as a king who wanted to gather all the wealth that his slaves were indebted to him for.
When the king started doing that, a man who owed him millions of dollars was brought to him.
The man was unable to pay. Therefore, his master gave an order. The man, his wife, his children, and everything he possessed were to be sold to pay his debt.
The servant knelt before him. "Give me time," he pleaded. "I will pay everything back."
His lord felt pity for him. He forgave him the debt he owed and set him free.
But the servant went out and found one of the other servants who owed him a few dollars. He grabbed him and began to strangle him. 'Pay me what you owe!' he said.
The other server fell to his knees. "Give me time," he pleaded with him. "I will repay you."
But the first servant refused. Instead, he went and threw the man into prison. He would be held there until he could repay his debt.
The other servants saw what happened. This disturbed them greatly. They went and told their master everything that had happened.
"Then the Lord called His first servant and said, 'You wicked servant! I forgave you all that debt you owed me because you appealed to me.'"
Haven't you had to show mercy to the other servant, just as you showed mercy to me?
Enraged, his master handed the prisoner over to his guards. He would be punished until he repaid all his debts.
Thus does my Father in heaven treat every one of you, if you do not forgive your brother from the bottom of your heart.
Jesus teaches about divorce.
When Jesus had finished saying these things, he left Galilee and went into the region of Judea on the other side of the Jordan River.
Large crowds followed him. He healed them there.
Some Pharisees came to test him. They asked, "Is it lawful for a man to divorce his wife for just any reason?"
Jesus answered, "Have you not read that God 'created them male and female' at the beginning? (Genesis 1:27)"
He said, "Therefore a man shall leave his father and mother and be joined to his wife, and they shall become one flesh" (Genesis 2:24).
What God has joined together, let no one separate.
They asked, "Why then did Moses command the man to write a divorce certificate for his wife and send her away?"
Jesus answered, "Moses allowed you to divorce your wives because your hearts were hard. But it was not like that in the beginning.
Here is what I say to you. Anyone who divorces his wife and marries another woman commits adultery. A man can only divorce his wife if she has been unfaithful to him.
The disciples said to them, "If that is the case with a man and his wife, it is better not to marry."
Jesus answered, "Not everyone can accept the idea of remaining single. Only those whom someone has helped to live without marrying can accept it.
Some men cannot have children because they were born that way. Some became that way because of other people. And others made themselves that way to serve the Kingdom of Heaven. And whoever can accept living that way, must do so." The little children come to Jesus
Some people brought two children to Jesus. They wanted him to lay his hands on the children and pray for them. But the disciples told the people to stop.
Jesus said, "Let the little children come to me, and do not hinder them, for the kingdom of God belongs to such as these."
Jesus laid his hands on them. Then he left from there. Jesus and the Rich Young Man
A man came to Jesus. And he asked, "Teacher, what shall I do that I may obtain eternal life?"
"Why do you ask me about what is good?" Jesus answered. "There is only one who is good. If you want to enter into the kingdom, obey the commandments."
"Which?" the man asked. Jesus said, "Do not murder. Do not commit adultery. Do not steal. Do not give false testimony against anyone."
Honor your father and your mother. and "You shall love your neighbor as yourself." (Leviticus 19:18)
"I have obeyed all those commands," said the young man. "What should I do next?"
Jesus answered, "If you want to be perfect, go, sell your possessions and give the money to the poor, and you will have treasure in heaven. Then come, follow me."
When the young man heard that, he left sadly. He was very wealthy.
Then Jesus said to his disciples: "What I now tell you is true. It is difficult for those who have wealth to enter the Kingdom of Heaven."
Again I tell you, it is easier for a camel to go through the eye of a needle than for someone who is rich to enter the kingdom of God.
When the disciples heard this, they were truly amazed. They asked, "Then who can be saved?"
Jesus looked at them and said, "What is impossible for man is possible for God."
Peter answered and said, "Behold, we have forsaken all to follow You! What then will be our reward?"
"I am not yet capable of saying to you that what I shall attain is the truth." Jesus said to them. "Therefore, if all things have become new, the Son of Man will sit down upon his glorious throne. And you who have followed me in the footsteps will sit down upon twelve thrones. And you will rule over the twelve tribes of Israel."
Everyone who has left a house or family or land because of me will receive 100 times as much. And they will also receive eternal life.
Many who are first will be last, and many who are last will be first.
The story of the workers in the vineyard.
The King of the Skies is like a man who has become a landowner. So he went out on top of the bakhoor tree to get a job for people in his field.
He agreed to pay them the usual wage for a day's work. Then he sent them into his vineyard.
He went out again around nine in the morning. He saw others standing in the marketplace square doing nothing.
He said to them, "You also go and work in my vineyards, and I will give you a fair wage."
They went out. "He went out again around noon and three and went out and did the same thing.
He went out around five and found others standing around. He asked them, "Why have you been standing here all day without doing anything?"
"Because no one of us has been hired," they answered. "He said to them, 'You go also to my harvest.'"
When evening came, the owner of the vineyard spoke to the manager of the workers. He said, "Call the workers and give them their pay. Start with the last ones. Then go to the first ones."
The workers who were hired came around five. Each of them received their usual day's wage.
Therefore, when those who had been hired first came, they expected to receive more. But each of them also received the pay for a normal day.
When they received it, they began complaining about the landlord.
'Those people you recently hired only worked for one hour,' they said. 'You paid them the same amount you paid us. We did most of the work and were under the hot sun all day.'
One of them the master answered: My friend, I am being fair with you. Did you not agree to work for the usual day's wage?
Take your money and go. I want to give the one I finally hired the same wage I gave you.
Do I not have the right to do what I want with my own money? Do you feel cheated because I gave others generously?
"Therefore the last will be first, and the first will be last." Jesus, once again, speaks of his coming death.
Jesus was going up to Jerusalem. And on the way, he took the twelve disciples aside to talk to them.
"We are going up to Jerusalem," he said. "The Son of Man will be delivered over to the chief priests and the scribes. They will condemn him to death."
Then they will deliver him to people who are not Jews. Those people will mock him and flog him. Then they will crucify him on a wooden beam. And on the third day, he will rise from the dead!" A woman asks Jesus for a favor.
The mother of the sons of Zebedee came to Jesus with her sons. She knelt down and asked him a favor.
"What do you want?" Jesus asked. "Promise me," she said, "that one of my sons will sit at your right in your kingdom. And promise me that the other will sit at your left."
"You do not know what you are asking," Jesus said to them. "Can you drink the cup that I am about to drink?" "Yes, we can," they answered.
Jesus said to them, "Assuredly, you will drink from my cup; and how I wish that none of you would drink it! But I have no choice but to accept the cup the Father has given me. And how I wish that none of you would have to drink from it! For my part, my Father is glorified by this. And he will glorify the Son, by whose name I have been given everything, from the Son of Man to the least of those who believe.' "
The other ten pupils heard about this. They were angry with the two brothers.
Jesus gathered them and said, "You know that the rulers exercise authority over people, and they dominate them, and they command them."
Do not be like that. Rather, if anyone among you wants to be great, he must be your servant.
And everyone who wants to be first must be a slave to you.
Be like the Son of Man. He did not come to be served, but to serve others. He came to give his life as a ransom for many people." Two blind men are given their sight.
Jesus and his disciples were leaving Jericho. A large crowd followed them.
There were two blind men sitting by the road. When they heard that Jesus was passing by, they called out, "Lord, Son of David, have mercy on us!"
The crowd ordered them to stop. They told them to be silent. But the two men shouted even louder, "Lord! Son of David! Have mercy on us!"
Jesus stopped and called to them. "What do you want me to do for you?" he asked.
"Our Lord," they answered, "we want to be able to see."
Jesus felt deep compassion for them. He touched their eyes, and they saw immediately. They followed him.
Jesus enters Jerusalem.
When they all approached Jerusalem, they came to Bethphage, which was on the Mount of Olives. Jesus sent two of his disciples.
And he said to them, "Go to the village before you. And as soon as you enter it, you will find a donkey tied up, and its foal is with it. Loose them and bring them to me."
If anyone tells you anything, say the Lord needs them. The Master will send them immediately.
This happened so that what was spoken through the prophet might be fulfilled. And it says,
"Tell this to the daughter of Zion: 'Behold, your king is coming to you, humble, and mounted on a donkey, and on a colt, the foal of a donkey.'" (Zechariah 9:9)
The disciples went and did what Jesus had commanded them.
They brought a donkey and a she-donkey. They put their cloaks on them. Then Jesus sat on the cloaks.
A very large crowd of people spread out on the road and opened their coats. Others cut branches from trees and spread them on the road.
Some of them went ahead of him, and some followed him. They all cried out: "Blessed is the Son of David! Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord!" (Psalm 118:26) "Blessed is he who is in the highest heavens!"
When Jesus entered Jerusalem, the whole city was stirred up. And the people asked, "Who is this?"
And the crowd answered, "This is Jesus of Nazareth, the prophet." Jesus Cleanses the Temple
Jesus entered the temple area. And he began to drive out all those who were buying and selling there. And he overturned the tables of the moneychangers, and the chairs of those who sold pigeons.
He said to them: "It is written that the Lord said: 'My house shall be a house of prayer for all peoples' (Isaiah 56:7) and 'you make it a den of thieves'" (Jeremiah 7:11).
The blind and those who suffered from deformities came to Jesus at the Temple. He healed them there.
The chief priests and the teachers of the law saw the miracles he was doing, and the children shouting in the temple area, "Praise to the Son of David!" But when the chief priests and teachers of the law saw this, they began to plot how to kill him.
"Do you hear what these children are saying?" they asked him. "Yes," Jesus answered. "Have you not read in the Scriptures: 'The very young will praise you, both the young men and the women, will offer you their praise.' (Psalm 8:2)"
Then Jesus went out from the crowd and went into the house of Simon the leper. He spent the night there. The branch of the vine decays.
Early in the morning, Jesus was going to Jerusalem. He was hungry.
He saw a fig tree by the road. He went up to it, but he found nothing on it except leaves. Then he said to it, "May you never bear fruit!" And the fig tree dried up immediately.
When the disciples saw this, they were perplexed. "How did the olive tree dry up so quickly?" they asked.
Jesus answered, "What I am about to tell you is true. You must believe and not doubt, and then you will be able to do what was done to the olive tree. And you can say to this mountain, 'Go and throw yourself into the sea,' and it will be done."
"If you believe, you will receive whatever you ask for in prayer." Doubting Jesus' authority
Jesus entered the temple courts. As he taught there, the chief priests and the scribes came to him, along with the elders, and asked him, "By what authority are you doing these things? And who gave you this authority?"
Jesus answered, "And I also will ask you a question. If you answer me, then I will tell you by what authority I do these things."
From where did the baptism of John come? From heaven or from men?" And they began to say to one another, "If we say, 'From heaven,' he will say, 'Why then did you not believe him?'
But what if we said, "of the men"? We are afraid of people. Everyone thought that John was a prophet.
They answered Jesus, "We do not know." Jesus said, "Neither will I tell you by what authority I do these things. The Parable of the Two Sons."
What do you think about this? A man had two sons. He went to the first son and said, "My son, I am going to work today in the vineyard."
"I will not," the son answered. But afterwards he changed his mind and went.
Then the father went to the other son. He told him the same thing. The son replied, "I will do it, Father." But he did not go.
Which of the two sons did the will of his father?" They said, "The first." Jesus said to them, "Truly I tell you, the tax collectors and sinners will enter the kingdom of God before you."
John came to show you the right way to live. But you did not believe him. But the tax collectors and gypsies believed him. You saw it. But until that time you had not turned away from your sins and believed him. The story of the tenants.
Listen to another story. There was a man who owned some land, so he planted vineyards. He put up a wall around them. And he dug a well to make wine in. He also built a watchtower. And he rented out the vineyards to some farmers. Then he went off on a journey.
When the harvest time approached, he sent his servants to the tenants. And he told the servants to collect his share of the fruits.
But the tenants seized his servants. They beat one of them. They killed another. They threw a stone at a third, intending to kill him.
Then the man sent other servants to the tenants. He sent more than he had sent at first. The tenants treated them in the same way.
And finally, He sent His Son to them and said, "They will respect my Son."
But the tenants saw the son coming. They said to each other, "This is the one who will take possession of the owner's property someday. Let's kill him. Then everything will be ours."
And they took him and cast him out of the vineyard. Then they killed him.
So what does the owner of the vineyard do when he comes back? Who are these renters?
"They will tear down those evil people's house," they answered. "Then the field will be rented to others. They will give him his share of the harvest at the time of the harvest."
Jesus said to them, "Have they not read in the Scriptures: 'The stone which the builders rejected has become the head of the corner. This is the Lord's doing; it is marvelous in our eyes'? (Psalm 118:22,23)"
Here is what I say to you. The kingdom of God will be taken from you, and it will be given to a people that produces its fruits.
Everyone who falls on that rock will be broken into pieces. But the rock will crush anyone it falls on.
The chief priests and the Pharisees heard reports about Jesus. They knew he was speaking about them.
Therefore, they looked for a way to arrest him. But they were afraid of the crowd. The people believed that Jesus was a prophet.
The Wedding Dinner Story
Jesus told them more stories. He said:
Here is how the Kingdom of Heaven is like. A king prepared a wedding feast for his son.
Servants were sent to those who had been invited to dinner. The servants told them to come. But they refused.
Then he sent more servants than before. He said, "Tell those who are invited that I have prepared my dinner. I have killed the sheep and the fattened cattle. Everything is ready." Then he added, "Come to the wedding feast."
But the people did not care. One of them went to his field. And another went to his work.
The rest of his servants seized him. They treated him badly and then killed him.
The king was furiously angry. He sent his army to destroy them. They killed those murderers and burned their city.
"Then the king said to his servants, 'The reading for the wedding has been prepared. But those whom I invited were not worthy to be present.'"
Go to the street corners. Invite to dinner anyone you find.
The servants went out into the streets. They gathered all the people they could find, both the good and the bad. Soon the wedding hall was filled with guests.
The king entered to see the guests. He noticed a man there who was not wearing wedding clothes.
"My friend," he said, "how did you get in here without your wedding clothes?" The man could not find what to say.
Then the king gestured to his slaves, so they shackled his hands and feet and took him outside where they cast him into the darkness. And there people weep and gnaw on their teeth.
Many are called, but few are chosen. Is it right to pay taxes to Caesar?
The Pharisees went out and plotted how they might entangle Jesus in His own words.
They sent their disciples to him. They sent the Herodians with them. "Teacher," they said, "we know that you are a man of integrity. You teach the way of God in truth. You do not allow others to tell you what you should do or say. You do not care how important they are."
Tell us then, what do you think? Is it right to pay taxes to Caesar or not?
But Jesus knew their evil intentions. He said to them, "You liars! Why are you trying to ensnare me?"
"Show me the money that people use to pay taxes." They brought him a silver coin.
He asked them, "Whose picture is this? And whose words are these?"
"Caesar," they answered. Then he said to them, "Render to Caesar the things that are Caesar's, and to God the things that are God's."
When they heard this, it was difficult for them. So they left, still hesitating. A marriage when the dead rise.
On the same day, Sadducees came to Jesus with a question. They do not believe that people rise from the dead.
"Teacher," they said, "this is what Moses told us. If a man dies without having children, his brother shall marry his widow. He shall have children who will carry his name."
There were seven brothers among us. The first got married and died. Since he had no children, he left his wife for his brother.
The same thing happened to the second brother, and the third brother. It even happened to the seventh brother.
Finally, the woman died.
Well, when the dead rise, who will be his wife? All seven were married to her.
Jesus answered, "You are mistaken, because you do not know the Scriptures, nor the power of God."
When the dead rise, they will not marry, nor will men give their daughters to them in marriage. They will be like the angels in heaven.
And what about the resurrection of the dead? Have you not read what God has said to you?
He said, "I am the God of Abraham, the God of Isaac, the God of Jacob" (Exodus 3:6) "Not the God of the dead, but the God of the living."
When the crowd heard this, they were amazed at his teaching. The most important commandment is...
The Pharisees heard that the Sadducees could not answer Jesus. So the Pharisees gathered together.
One of them, who was a lawyer, stood up and put Jesus to the test with a question.
"Teacher," he asked, "which commandment in the law is the most important?"
Jesus answered: 'Blessed are the meek, for they shall inherit the earth. Blessed are the peacemakers, for they shall be called sons of God. Blessed are they which do hunger and thirst after righteousness, for they shall be filled. Blessed are the merciful, for they shall obtain mercy. Blessed are the pure in heart, for they shall see God. Blessed are the peacemakers, for they shall be called sons of God. Blessed are they which do hunger and thirst after righteousness, for they shall be filled. Blessed are the merciful, for they shall obtain mercy. Blessed are the pure in heart, for they shall see God. Blessed are the peacemakers, for they shall be called sons of God. Blessed are they which do hunger and thirst after righteousness, for they shall be filled. Blessed are the merciful, for they shall obtain mercy. Blessed are the pure in heart, for they shall see God. Blessed are the peacemakers, for they shall be called sons of God. Blessed are they which do hunger and thirst after righteousness, for they shall be filled. Blessed are the merciful, for they shall obtain mercy. Blessed are the pure in heart, for they shall see God. Blessed are the peacemakers, for they shall be called sons of God. Blessed are they which do hunger and thirst after righteousness, for they shall be filled. Blessed are the merciful, for they shall obtain mercy. Blessed are the pure in heart, for they shall see God. Blessed are the peacemakers, for they shall be called sons of God. Blessed are they which do hunger and thirst after righteousness, for they shall be filled. Blessed are the merciful, for they shall obtain mercy. Blessed are the pure in heart, for they shall see God. Blessed are the peacemakers, for they shall be called sons of God. Blessed are they which do hunger and thirst after righteousness, for they shall be filled. Blessed are the merciful, for they shall obtain mercy. Blessed are the pure in heart, for they shall see God. Blessed are the peacemakers, for they shall be called sons of God. Blessed are they which do hunger and thirst after righteousness, for they shall be filled. Blessed are the merciful, for they shall obtain mercy. Blessed are the pure in heart, for they shall see God. Blessed are the peacemakers, for they shall be called sons of God. Blessed are they which do hunger and thirst after righteousness, for they shall be filled. Blessed are the merciful, for they shall obtain mercy. Blessed are the pure in heart, for they shall see God. Blessed are the peacemakers, for they shall be called sons of God. Blessed are they which do hunger and thirst after righteousness, for they shall be filled. Blessed are the merciful, for they shall obtain mercy. Blessed are the pure in heart, for they shall see God. Blessed are the peacemakers, for they shall be called sons of God. Blessed are they which do hunger and thirst after righteousness, for they shall be filled. Blessed are the merciful, for they shall obtain mercy. Blessed are the pure in heart, for they shall see God. Blessed are the peacemakers, for they shall be called sons of God. Blessed are they which do hunger and thirst after righteousness, for they shall be filled. Blessed are the merciful, for they shall obtain mercy. Blessed are the pure in heart, for they shall see God. Blessed are the peacemakers, for they shall be called sons of God. Blessed are they which do hunger and thirst after righteousness, for they shall be filled. Blessed are the merciful, for they shall obtain mercy. Blessed are the pure in heart, for they shall see God. Blessed are the peacemakers, for
This is the first and most important commandment.
And the second is like it: 'Love your neighbor as yourself.' (Leviticus 19:18)
All that was written in the law and the prophets, was written on the basis of these two commandments." Who is the Son of Man?
The Pharisees met. Jesus asked them,
"What do you think about the Christ? Whose son is he?" They answered, "He is the son of David."
He said to them, "Then why does David call him 'Lord'?" The Holy Spirit spoke through David himself. David said,
'The Lord said to my Lord, "Sit at my right hand, until I make your enemies your footstool" (Psalm 110:1)'
So how can David be his son when he calls him "my lord"?
No one could answer him with a single word. And from that day on, no one dared to ask him any questions.
Jesus condemns the Pharisees and teachers of the law.
Jesus spoke to the crowds and to his disciples.
He said: "He will sit in the council of Moses, the Pharisees, and the scribes."
Therefore you must obey them. Do everything they ask of you. But do not do what they do. They do not practice what they preach.
They burden others with their responsibilities, yet they themselves are unwilling to lift a finger to help carry them.
All they do is done so that others may see it. And on their foreheads and arms they wear small boxes containing texts from the Holy Book. And they make the boxes very wide. And they make the beads on their coats very long.
They love to sit in the place of honor at banquets. They also love to be in the most important seats in temples.
They like to be welcomed into the markets. They like it when they are called "the Lord".
But you should not be called "Rabbanites." You have one Father, and you are all brothers.
Do not say "father" to anyone on earth. For you have one Father, and He is in heaven.
You should not be called "teachers". You have one teacher, the Christ.
The most important person among you is your servant.
He who elevates himself will be pulled up, and he who is pulled up will be raised.
"How bad it will be for you, teachers of the law and the prophets! You are hypocrites! You lock up the kingdom of heaven in men's faces. You do not enter, nor do you allow those who would enter to do so."
<.>
"How awful it is for you, teachers of the law and you, Pharisees! Hypocrites! You cross sea and land to make one convert; and once you have made him a convert, you make him twice as much a child of hell as you are yourselves."
"How terrible it is for you, blind guides! For you say, 'If anyone swears by the temple, it means nothing. But if anyone swears by the gold in the temple, they must keep their oath.'"
You are blind and stupid! What is more important? The gold? Or is it the temple that makes the gold sacred?
You also say: "He who swears by the altar, his oath is vain, but he who swears by the gift which is offered upon it, of good things, his oath stands."
Uncle! Which is more important? Is it the gift? Or the altar that makes the gift sacred?
For whoever swears by the altar, swears by it and by everything on it.
And whoever swears by the place of his dwelling swears by it and by the one who dwells in it.
And everyone who swears by heaven swears by the name of God's throne and by the one who sits on it.
"How terrible it will be for you, teachers of the law and you, Pharisees! You are hypocrites! You give God a tenth of your spices, such as mint, dill, and cumin. But you have not practiced the more important matters of the law, such as justice, mercy, and faithfulness. You should have practiced the latter without neglecting the former."
O ye guides of guides! Ye remove the smallest insect from your food. But ye swallow whole camels!
Woe to you, teachers of the law and you, Pharisees, hypocrites! You are like tombs, which look beautiful on the outside but inside are full of dead bones and everything unclean. Even so, on the outside you appear righteous to people, but inside you are full of hypocrisy and lawlessness.
Freshness is blind! First I clean the inside of the glass and the plate. Then the outside is also clean.
How terrible it is for you, teachers of the law and you, Pharisees, you hypocrites! You are like whitewashed tombs. They look beautiful on the outside, but inside, they are full of the bones of the dead and also full of other things that are not pure and clean.
This is the case with you. From the outside, it seems that you are doing what is right. But on the inside, you are full of what is wrong. You pretend to be what you are not.
"How terrible it will be for you teachers of the law and you Pharisees hypocrites! You build tombs for the prophets and you decorate the tombs of the righteous.
And you would say, "If we had been with them, we would not have been slain with them, along with the prophets."
So you are testifying against yourselves. You are admitting that you are the sons of those who killed the prophets.
So complete what those before you from among the disobedience began!
O nest of poisonous vipers! How will you escape the torment in hell?
Therefore, I am sending you prophets and wise men and teachers. You will kill some of them, you will crucify others, and still others you will flog in your synagogues and persecute from town to town.
Therefore, you will pay the price for all the innocent people's blood that has been shed on the earth. I mean, from the blood of Abraham, the father of Ephron, to the blood of Zechariah, the son of Berekiah. Zechariah is the one whom you killed between the temple and the altar.
What I am going to tell you is a reality. All of this is going to happen to those who are living today.
Jerusalem! Jerusalem! You who kill the prophets and stone those sent to you. How often I have longed to gather your people together as a hen gathers her brood under her wings. But you would not let me!
Look, your house is empty.
I tell you, you will not see me again until you say, "Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord." (Psalm 118:26)
End of the world signs
Jesus left the temple. He was walking away when his disciples came to him. They wanted to draw his attention to the temple buildings.
"Do you see all these things?" Jesus asked. "What I tell you is true. Not one stone will be left on top of another. Every stone will be thrown down."
Jesus was sitting on the Mount of Olives. There, the disciples came to him privately. "Tell us," they said, "when will this happen? And what is the sign of your coming? And what is the sign of the end?"
Jesus answered, "Beware! Take care that no one deceives you."
Many will come in my name. They will say, "I am the Christ!" They will deceive many people.
You will hear about wars. You will hear people speaking about wars in the future. Do not be troubled. These things should happen. But the end has not come yet.
There will be war between nation and nation. Kingdom will fight against kingdom. People will suffer from hunger. Earthquakes will happen in many places.
All these are the beginning of the birth pangs.
Then they will hand you over to be afflicted, and to be killed. And you will be a burden on every nation, a contribution from you.
At that time, many will turn away from their faith. They will hate each other. They will hand each other over to their enemies.
Many false prophets will appear. They will deceive many people.
Because evil grows, the love of most people will turn cold.
But whoever endures to the end will be saved.
This proclamation from our kingdom will be published throughout the world. You will be a witness to all nations. Then the end will come.
The prophet Daniel spoke of "the detestable thing that causes desolation" (Daniel 9:27; 11:31; 12:11). You will see it standing in the holy place one day. Let the reader understand.
Then those in Galilee will go out to the mountains.
Nobody on the roof goes down to his house to take something.
Nobody in the square should go back to get their coat.
How terrible that would be for pregnant women! How terrible that would be for nursing mothers!
Pretend that you will not have to flee in winter or on a Saturday.
There will be terrible suffering in those days. It will be worse than anything else that has ever happened in the world since the beginning of time. And there will never be anything like it again.
If time were not shortened, no one would survive. But because of the chosen people of God, it will be shortened.
At that time, someone will say to you, 'Look! Here is the Christ!' or 'He is here!' Do not believe it.
False Messiahs and prophets will appear. They will do great signs and miracles. They will seek to deceive God's chosen people if possible.
Look, I already told you.
Therefore, if anyone tells you, "He is far off in the desert," do not go there. Or if anyone tells you, "He is deep within the house," do not believe it.
The lightning that comes from the east can be seen in the west. It will be the same when the Son of Man comes.
Vultures will gather wherever a carcass is found.
After the terrible suffering of those days, 'the moon will be confounded, and the sun ashamed; for the moon will be confounded, and the sun ashamed; for the stars of heaven will be turned to dust, and the whole constellation of heaven will be dissolved and fall, and the signboards of the heavens will be completely overthrown.' (Isaiah 13:10; 34:4)
"Then the sign of the Son of Man will appear in heaven, and all the nations of the earth will mourn. They will see the Son of Man coming on the clouds of heaven, with power and great glory.
He will send his angels with the cry of a trumpet of loud sounding. He will gather his chosen people from all the quarters. They will come with them from the farthest ends of the heavens to the last.
Learn from the fig tree. Once its branches become soft and its leaves sprout, you know that summer is near.
Likewise, when you see all those things happening, you know the end is near. It's at the door.
What I am about to tell you is true. And people living at that time will not die until those things happen.
The heaven and the earth shall be rolled up, but my word shall not be rolled up.
No one knows that day or hour. Not even the angels in heaven know. The Son does not know. Only the Father knows.
Remember how the affairs of this world were in the days of Noah. So will it be when the Son of Man comes.
In the days before the flood, people were eating and drinking. They were marrying. They were giving their daughters in marriage. They were doing all those things until the day that Noah entered the ark.
They did not know what was going to happen until the flood came and took them all. It will be the same when the Son of Man comes.
There will be two men in a field. One will be taken and the other left.
Two women will grind using a hand mill. One will be taken and the other will be left.
So fear, for you do not know on what day your Lord will come to you.
You must understand something. Imagine that the homeowner knows at what time of night the thief comes. Then he would keep watch on his house. He would not leave his house open.
Therefore you also must be ready; for the Son of Man will come at an hour when you do not expect.
Imagine a master who put one of his servants in charge of the other servants in his house. The servant's job is to give them their food at the proper time. The master wants a faithful and wise servant for this.
It would be good for the servant if the master found him doing his work when the master returned.
What I am going to tell you is true. This servant will put the master in charge of everything he owns.
But what if this servant was evil. What if he said to himself: "My master will be gone for a long time.
Suppose he began to strike the other servants. And suppose that he eats and drinks with those who drink heavily.
The master of that slave will come one day that the slave does not expect. He will return in an hour that the slave does not know.
Then his master will cut him into pieces and send him to the place where flatterers go. There people will weep and gnash their teeth.
The Story of the Ten Brides
Here is the picture of the kingdom of the heavens at that time. Fifteen virgins took their lamps and went out to meet the bridegroom.
Five of them were foolish. And five were wise.
The fools took their lamps, but they did not take olive oil with them.
The wise ones took oil in vessels with their lamps.
The groom didn't come for a long time. So all the bridesmaids slept and slept.
In the middle of the night, someone shouted: "Here is the groom! Come to meet him!"
Then all the bridesmaids of the young bride awoke and prepared their candles.
The foolish said to the wise, "Give us some of your oil. Our lamps are going out."
"'No,' they answered. 'There may not be enough for all of us. Instead, go to those who are selling the oil. Buy some for yourselves.'"
So they went to buy the oil. But while they were on their way, the bridegroom arrived. And the brides who had been getting ready with him entered the dining room. Then she closed the door.
After that, the other brides came. 'Sir! Sir!' they said. 'Open the door for us!'
But he answered, "What I am going to tell you are facts. I do not know you."
"Therefore, keep yourselves vigilant. You do not know the day or the hour when the bridegroom will come. The parable of three servants."
And again, here is what the kingdom of heaven will be like. A man traveling sent his servants to call workers for his vineyard.
He gave one of them 10 thousand dollars. He gave another one 4 thousand dollars. He gave the third one 2 thousand dollars. The man gave each servant the amount of money he knew the servant could handle. Then he continued his journey.
The server who received the $10,000 went immediately and put his money to work. He earned another $10,000.
The one who won 4000 dollars won 4000 dollars.
But the man who received 2000 dollars went and dug a hole in the ground. And he hid his master's money there.
After a long time, the master of these servants returned. He wanted to collect all the money that they had earned.
The man who received 10 thousand dollars brought the other 10 thousand. He said: "Sir, you entrusted me with 10 thousand dollars. Look, I have earned another 10 thousand dollars."
His master answered, "Well done, good and faithful servant! You have been faithful with a few things; I will put you in charge of many things. Come and share your master's happiness!"
The man who came had about 4000 dollars. He said: "Sir, you have entrusted me with 4000 dollars. Look, I have earned another 4000 dollars."
His master answered, "Well done, good and faithful servant! You have been faithful with a few things; I will put you in charge of many things. Come and share your master's happiness!"
Then the man who had received the two thousand dirhams came. He said, "O my lord, I knew that you were a severe man. You reap where you did not sow. You gather the harvest where you did not scatter the seed."
I was afraid. I went out and hid $2000 for you in the ground. Look, this is yours.
Then his master said to him, "You wicked, lazy servant! Did you know that I harvest where I did not sow, and I gather where I did not scatter?
Well, you should have put my money in the bank. When I came back, I would have received it with interest.
"Then his master ordered the other servants, 'Take 2,000 dollars from him. Give it to the one who has 20,000 dollars.'
Here is the English translation: He who has will be given more. He will have more than enough. And what about someone who does not have? Even what he has will be taken away from him.
Throw this good-for-nothing servant into the outer darkness. There men will weep and grind their teeth.
