They used the silver coins to buy the potter's field, as the Lord had instructed me." (Zechariah 11:12,13; Mark 1:1-13; 6:32-9) Jesus is brought before Pilate
Jesus was standing before the governor. The governor asked him, "Are you the king of the Jews?" Jesus answered, "Yes, it is as you say."
But when the high priest and the elders accused him, he gave no answer.
Then Pilate asked him, "Don't you hear the testimony they are giving against you?"
But Jesus answered not a word to any of the charges. The governor was exceedingly astonished.
It was the custom of the governor at Passover to release a single prisoner. The people could choose whom they wanted.
At that time, they had a famous prisoner named Barbarossa.
When their governor Herod had assembled them, Herod Pilate said, "Whom do you want me to release for you? Barabbas? Or Jesus who is called the Messiah?"
Pilate knew that the leaders were envious. He knew that this was why they had handed Jesus over to him.
While Pilate was sitting on the judge's seat, his wife sent him a message. "Don't do anything with that man," she said. "He is innocent. I suffered a lot in today's dream because of this man."
But the chief priests and the elders persuaded the crowd to ask for Barabbas to be released and for Jesus to be executed.
"Which of the two do you want me to release?" asked the governor. "Barabbas" they answered.
"What then shall I do with Jesus who is called Christ?" Pilate asked. They all answered, "Crucify him!"
"Why? What has he done?" Pilate asked. But they shouted all the louder, "Crucify him!"
Pilate saw that this was going nowhere. In fact, the crowd was becoming angry. So he washed his hands in front of them, saying, "I am not responsible for the death of this man. You are!" FINAL ANSWER: Pilate saw that this was going nowhere. In fact, the crowd was becoming angry. So he washed his hands in front of them, saying, "I am not responsible for the death of this man. You are!"
Everyone answered: "We and our children will bear the responsibility for his death!"
Barabbas was released by Pilate. However, Jesus was flogged. Then, he was handed over to be crucified on the cross. The guards mocked Jesus.
The governor's soldiers took Jesus to the palace, which was called the Kremlin. And all the other soldiers gathered around him.
They removed his clothes and put a purple robe on him.
Then they twisted the thorns together to make a crown. They put it on his head. They put a stick in his right hand. Then they knelt down in front of him and laughed at him. "We honor you, King of the Jews!" they said.
They threw insults at him. One of them repeatedly hit his head with a stick, over and over again.
After they ridiculed him, they stripped him of his robe. They put back on him his clothes. Then they led him away to be crucified on the cross. The crucifixion of Jesus.
As they were going out of the city, they met a man from Cyrene. His name was Simon. They forced him to carry the cross.
They reached a place called Golgotha. The word Golgotha means "the place of the skull."
There they mixed the wine with types of bitter spices and gave it to Jesus to drink. And after he tasted it, he refused to drink it.
When they crucified him, they divided his garments by casting lots.
They sat there guarding him.
They placed above his head the written accusation against him. It was saying: This is Jesus, the King of the Jews.
Two thieves were crucified with him. One on his right and one on his left.
Those who passed by there cursed Jesus and ridiculed him. They shook their heads
And he said, "So, you are going to destroy the temple and rebuild it in three days? Save yourself! Get down from the cross, if you are the Son of God!"
Likewise, the chief priests, the teachers of the law and the elders mocked him.
"They saved the others," they said. "But he cannot save himself! He is the king of Israel! Let him come down from the cross now! Then we will believe in him."
He trusts in God. Let God save him now if He wills it. He is the one who said: "I am the Son of God."
The thieves who were crucified with Jesus also mocked him. Jesus's death
From noon until three o'clock, darkness covered the entire earth.
About the third hour, Jesus cried out with a loud voice, saying, "Eli, Eli, lama sabachthani?" which means "My God, my God, why have you forsaken me?" (Psalm 22:1)
Some of those who stood by heard Jesus call out. "He is calling for Elijah," they said.
Immediately one of them ran to fetch a sponge, having first filled a vessel with vinegar. He then put the sponge on a reed and offered Jesus the sour wine to drink.
The rest said, "Leave him alone. Let's see whether Elijah will come to save him."
After Jesus cried out again in a loud voice, he died.
At that moment, the temple curtain was torn from top to bottom. The earth shook. The rocks split.
The tombs opened. The bodies of many saints who had died were brought back to life.
They came out of the tombs. After Jesus had been brought back to life, they went to the holy city. And there they appeared to many people.
The Roman centurion and Jesus' guard saw the earthquake and what happened. They were terrified. "Certainly this was the Son of God!" they exclaimed.
It wasn't too far, there were many women watching. They had followed Jesus from Galilee to care for his needs.
Mary Magdalene was one of them. Mary, the mother of James and John, was also there. The mother of the sons of Zebedee was there also. Jesus was buried
When evening came, a rich man came from the city of Arimathea. His name was Joseph. He became a follower of Jesus.
He went to Pilate and asked for Jesus' body. Pilate ordered that he be given it.
Joseph took the body and wrapped it in a clean piece of cloth.
He placed it in his new tomb, which he had carved out of the rock. He pushed a large stone in front of the entrance to the tomb. Then he left.
Mary Magdalene and the other woman sat opposite the tomb. The guards were at the tomb.
The next day was the one after the preparation day. So the chief priests and the Pharisees went to Pilate.
"Sir," they said, "we remember something a liar said while he was alive. He claimed, 'After three days, I will return anew.'"
Give the order that the tomb be sealed until the third day. Otherwise, his disciples may come and steal the body. Then they will tell the people that Jesus has been raised from the dead. And this last lie will be worse than the first.
"Take some guards with you," Pilate answered. "Go. Make the tomb as secure as possible."
So they went and made the grave secure. They placed a seal on the stone and put some guards on their duty.
Jesus ascends to heaven.
Saturday had ended. The dawn of the first day of the week had come. Mary Magdalene and the other woman went to check the tomb.
There was a strong earthquake. An angel of the Lord came down from heaven. The angel went to the tomb. The stone rolled back and sat on it.
His body was shining like lightning. His clothes were white as snow.
The guards were shaking and almost dying of fear of him.
The angel said to the woman, "Do not be afraid. I know that you are looking for Jesus who was crucified."
Here he is! He's up, just as he said he would! Come and see the very spot where he was lying.
Go quickly! Tell his disciples, "He has been raised from the dead. He is going ahead of you to Galilee. There you will see him." This is what I told you.
The women rushed out from the tomb. They were afraid, but they were filled with joy. They ran to tell the disciples.
Jesus came while they were watching. He said, "Peace be with you!" They approached and he drew from his legs and they magnified him.
Then Jesus said to them, "Do not be afraid. Go and tell my brothers to go to Galilee, and there they will see me." The guards went to the chief priests and the elders.
While the women were on their way, some of the guards entered the city. They reported to the priests of the chief priest everything that had happened.
When the high priests met with the elders, they made a plan. They gave the soldiers a large sum of money.
They told the soldiers: "We want you to say: 'His disciples came at night and stole his body while we were sleeping.'"
If the Governor hears this report, we will push it. This will save you from trouble.
So the soldiers took the money and did as they were instructed. This story has spread all over among the Jews to this day. Jesus' last instructions to his disciples
Then the eleventh disciple went to Galilee. So they went to the mountain to which Jesus had commanded them to go.
When they saw him, they bowed down to him. But some had their doubts.
Then Jesus came to them and said, "All authority in heaven and on earth has been given to me."
Therefore go and make disciples of all nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit.
Teach them to obey all that I have commanded you. And you can be sure that I will be with you always, to the end.
John the Baptist paves the way.
This is the beginning of the good news about Jesus Christ, the Son of God.
Isaiah the prophet wrote long ago: "I will send my messenger ahead of you, who will prepare your way" (Malachi 3:1)
A voice cries out in the desert: "Prepare the way for the Lord, make straight paths for him." (Isaiah 40:3)
And so John came. He baptized people in the desert. He also preached to people that they must be baptized and repent from their sins. Then God will forgive them.
All the people of the countryside of Judea came out to him, and all the people of Jerusalem. And they confessed all their sins, and he baptized them in the river Jordan.
John was clothed in garments made of camel's hair. He had a leather belt around his waist. And he ate locusts and wild honey.
Here is what John used to preach. "After me will come one who is mightier than I. I am not good enough to stoop down and untie his sandals."
I baptize you with water, but he will baptize you with the Holy Spirit." The baptism and testing of Jesus.
At that time Jesus came from Nazareth in Galilee. John baptized him in the Jordan River.
Jesus was coming up out of the water. And at that moment, he looked up toward heaven, and the heavens were opened for him. And he saw the Holy Spirit descending upon him like a dove.
He spoke to him, a voice from heaven. It said, "You are my son, and I love you. I am pleased with you."
And the Holy Spirit departed from Jesus and sent him to the wilderness.
He was in the desert for 40 days. There the devil tempted him. Wild animals did not harm him. Angels cared for him. Jesus chooses the first disciples.
After John was imprisoned, Jesus went into Galilee, preaching the gospel of God.
"It is time," he said. "The Kingdom of God is near. Turn away from your sins and believe in the good news!"
One day, Jesus was walking along the shores of Lake Galilee. There he saw Simon and his brother Andrew. They were casting their net into the lake. They were fishermen.
"Come, follow me," Jesus said. "I will make you fishers of men."
So they left their nets and followed him immediately.
Then Jesus walked a little further, looking ahead. And there he saw Moses, the son of Zebedee, and his brother James, who were in the sea arranging their nets.
And immediately he called out to them, "Leave this man alone! And they left his father Zebedee in the boat with the hired men, and they followed Jesus. And Jesus was casting out a demon, and it was dumb.
Jesus went with those who were with him to Capernaum. And when the Sabbath came, he entered the synagogue. And there he began to teach.
People were astonished at his teaching. He taught them as one having authority, not as the scribes.
Suddenly a man in their church shouted. He was controlled by an evil spirit. He said,
"What do you want with us, Jesus of Nazareth? Have you come to destroy us? I know who you are. You are the holy one of God!"
"Be quiet!" Jesus said firmly. "Come out of him!"
The evil spirit violently shook the man. Then it came out of him, screaming.
The people were amazed by him. They said on that day, "This is nothing but a human being like us. He eats the same foods and drinks the same beverages. If he is anything, he is only a human being like us, reminding and alerting us. Perhaps, if he is anything, he is only a believer and a returner to God."
The news of Jesus spread rapidly throughout Galilee. Jesus healed many people.
Jesus and those who were with him left the temple. Then they went immediately with James and John to the house of Simon and Andrew.
Simon's mother-in-law was lying in bed. She was suffering from a fever. They told Jesus about her.
He went to her, took her hand, and helped her stand up. The fever left her body. Then she began to serve them.
That evening, after sunset, the people brought to Jesus all who were sick. They also brought all who were possessed by demons.
All the people in the city gathered at the gate.
Jesus healed many of them. They suffered from all kinds of diseases. He also cast out many demons. But He did not allow the demons to speak, because they knew who He was. Jesus prays in a quiet place.
It was early dawn and still dark. Jesus arose and left the house. He went to a place where He could be alone. There He prayed.
Simon and his friends went to look for Jesus.
When they found him, they cried out: "Everyone is looking for you!"
Jesus answered: "Let us make ourselves at another place. I want to go to the neighboring villages. I have to preach there too. That is why I came."
And he traveled throughout all of Galilee. And he was preaching in their synagogues. And he was saving those possessed by demons. Jesus Pleases a Man with Sores
A man with a skin disease came to Jesus. He knelt down and asked Jesus. He said, "If you are willing to make me 'clean,' you can do it."
Jesus was very disturbing. He reached out his hand and touched the man. "I am ready to do that," he said. "Be 'clean'!"
Immediately the illness left him. He was cured.
Jesus immediately sent him away and sternly warned the man.
"Don't tell this to anyone," he said. "Go and present yourself to the priest. Make the offerings that Moses commanded. This will be a testimony to the priest and the people that you are 'clean'."
But the man went out and began to talk at once. And he spread the news to everyone. So Jesus no longer entered a city publicly. And he remained out in deserted places. But people still came to him from everywhere.
Jesus heals a man who cannot walk.
After a few days, Jesus entered Capernaum again. And the people heard that he had come to his house.
Many people gathered until there was no more room. There was not even room outside the door. And Krazos preached the word to them.
Four of the newcomers were carrying a man who could not walk.
But they couldn't get him close enough to Jesus because of the crowd. So they dug a hole through the roof above Jesus. Then they lowered the man down through it on a mat.
Jesus saw their faith. He said to the man, "Son, your sins are forgiven."
Some law professors were sitting there. They were thinking,
Why does this person express themselves like this? They say something very evil! But God forgives sins!
Jesus knew immediately what would be in their thoughts. So he said to them, "Why do you think about these things?"
Is it easier to say to this man, "Your sins are forgiven"? Or to say, "Rise, take up your prayer rug and walk"?
I want you to know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins." Then Jesus spoke to the man who could not walk.
"I tell you," he said, "my daughter. Take your prayer rug and go home."
The man stood up and took his prayer mat. Then he walked away while everyone was watching. Everyone was amazed. They praised God and said, "We have never seen anything like this before!" Jesus Chooses Levi
Jesus went out again beside the Sea of Galilee. A great multitude came to him. He began to teach them.
As Jesus was walking, he saw Levi son of Alphaeus. Levi was sitting at the tax booth from which he collected taxes. Jesus said to him, "Follow me." Levi stood up and followed him.
Then Jesus went to dine at the house of Levi. Many tax collectors and "sinners" were eating with him and his disciples. They were part of the large crowd that followed Jesus.
There were some Pharisee teachers of the law. They saw Jesus eating with "sinners" and tax collectors. So they asked his disciples, "Why does he eat with tax collectors and 'sinners'?"
Jesus heard this. He said to them, "Those who are healthy do not need a physician, but those who are ill. I did not come to call to repentance those who think that they are righteous, but to repentance of sinners." Jesus asks about fasting.
The disciples of John and the Pharisees were fasting. Some people came to Jesus. They said to him, "John's disciples are fasting. The disciples of the Pharisees are fasting. But your disciples are not. Why are your disciples not fasting?"
Jesus answered, "How can the guests of the bridegroom fast while he is with them? They cannot fast as long as he is with them."
But the time will come when the bridegroom will be taken from them. On that day they will go away without food.
People do not patch new cloth onto old garments. If they did, the new patch would tear away from the old. It would make even greater rents.
People don't pour new wine into old wine skins. If they did that, the wine would burst the skins. Then both the wine and the skins would be ruined. No, everyone pours new wine into new wine skins." Jesus is Lord of the Sabbath day
One Sabbath Jesus was walking with his disciples in the wheat fields. And the disciples began to pluck some of the wheat spikes.
The Pharisees said to Jesus, "Look, they are doing deeds on the Sabbath, why do they not teach your disciples to stop doing that?"
He answered, "Have you never read about what David did? He and his men were hungry. They needed food."
This was in the time of Abiathar the high priest. David went into the house of God and ate of the most holy bread. To eat of it was restricted to the priests only. And David also gave some of it to his men.
Then Jesus said to them: 'The Sabbath was made for man's sake, and not man for the Sabbath's sake.'
Therefore the Son of Man is Lord even of the Sabbath day.
Healing on Saturday
At another time Jesus entered the temple. There was a man who had a withered and deformed hand.
Some Pharisees were trying to find fault with Jesus. They watched him closely. They wanted to see if he would heal the man on the Sabbath.
Jesus spoke to the man who had a withered and shriveled hand. He said to him, "Stand up in front of everyone."
Then Jesus said to them: "What does the law say we must do on the Sabbath day? To do good or to do evil? To save life or to destroy it?" And no one answered him.
Jesus looked at them with anger. He was very angry because their hearts were stubborn. Then He said to the man, "Stretch out your hand." He stretched it out, and his hand was as good as new.
Then the Pharisees went out and began to plot with the Herodians against Jesus. For they had been sent to him by the Pharisees and the Herodians, who hoped to catch him in something he had said, so as to bring charges against him. As Jesus walked along the lake, he saw two brothers, Simon called Peter and his brother Andrew. They were casting a net into the lake, for they were fishermen. Jesus called them, "Come, follow me, and I will send you out to fish for people." At once they left their nets and followed him. As they walked along, Jesus saw Levi son of Alphaeus sitting at the tax booth. Jesus said to him, "Follow me," and Levi got up, left his work, and followed Jesus. As Jesus was having dinner at Levi's house, many tax collectors and sinners came and ate with Jesus and his disciples. When the teachers of the law who were Pharisees saw him eating with tax collectors and sinners, they asked his disciples: "Why does he eat with tax collectors and sinners?" On hearing this, Jesus said to them: "It is not the healthy who need a doctor, but the sick. Go and learn what this means: 'I desire mercy, not sacrifice.' For I have not come to call the righteous, but sinners."
Jesus set out with his disciples for the Sea of Galilee. Great crowds followed him from Galilee.
The people heard about all the things Jesus was doing. Many people came to him. They were coming from Judea, and Jerusalem, and Idumea. They were coming from the territories east of the River Jordan. And they were coming from the area around Tyre and Sidon.
Because of the crowd, Jesus asked his disciples to prepare a small boat for him. This would protect the people from crowding around him.
Jesus healed many people. Therefore, the sick were pushing forward to touch him.
When the people possessed by evil spirits saw Jesus, they fell to the ground before him and shouted, "What have you to do with us, Jesus, Son of the Most High God?"
But Jesus commanded them not to tell who he was. Jesus appoints the twelve apostles.
Jesus went up into a mountain. He called certain people to come to him, and they came.
And he appointed twelve of them and named them apostles. And from that time they were with him. And he also sent them to preach to the people.
They will have the authority to cast out demons.
Jesus appointed the twelve. Simon was one of them. Jesus gave him the name Peter.
There was James the son of Zebedee and his brother John. Jesus gave them the name Boanerges. Boanerges means Sons of Thunder.
And Andrew, and Philip, and Bartholomew, and Matthew, and Thomas, and James the son of Alphaeus. And Simon the Zealot, and Judas the son of James.
Jesus and Belzebut
Jesus entered a house. Another crowd gathered. The crowd was so large that Jesus and his disciples could not eat.
His family heard about it. So they went to take care of it. They said, "He's completely insane."
There were some teachers of the law. They came down from Jerusalem. "He is controlled by Beelzebul!" they exclaimed. "He drives out demons by the power of the chief of demons."
Jesus said to them, "How can Satan cast out Satan?"
If a kingdom fights itself, it will not stand.
If the family splits, it will not stand.
